  "body": "### Proposal Details\n\nChange-Id: [I126c59ab2c8a0c9885b40691b8f082bf3bd15a55](https://go-review.googlesource.com/q/I126c59ab2c8a0c9885b40691b8f082bf3bd15a55)\n\nCurrently, `crypto/hkdf`'s `Expand` and `Key` functions accept `info` as a string, which requires conversions when using numeric counters or other binary metadata.\n\nProblems in `crypto/internal/fips140/hkdf.Expand`:\n\n```go\nexpander.Write([]byte(info))\nexpander.Write([]byte{counter})\n```\n\n1. Each iteration converts `info` to `[]byte`, causing unnecessary allocations.\n2. Most metadata is already in `[]byte`, so forcing a string conversion is awkward.\n3. The argument `info` is immutable; converting in a loop is wasteful in hot paths.\n4. Converting `counter` each loop iteration allocates unnecessarily; a single 1-byte buffer can be reused.\n\nUsing `ExpandBytes`, allocations can drop from O(N*2) to O(1) per HKDF call.\n\nThis proposal adds:\n\n1. `ExpandBytes` - like `Expand`, but accepts `info []byte`.\n2. `KeyBytes` - like `Key`, but accepts `info []byte`.\n3. `Expand` and `Key` are rewritten to call these functions while still accepting a string but converting it to `[]byte` once per call.\n4. Reuses a counter buffer and avoids repeated string conversions.\n\nOld functions remain for backwards compatibility.\n\n---\n\n### Motivation\n\n* Avoid repeated string conversions in performance-critical HKDF use.\n* Support `[]byte` metadata directly (counters, file hashes).\n* Minimal API change, fully backward-compatible.",
